  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nWell advertised return to periodically wet/stormy wx on track for \nthis weekend as another moisture laden frontal zone sets up over the \narea. Convectively enhanced mid level perturbations tracking east \ninto the lower Great Lakes, in tandem with several bouts of moisture \ntransport/convergence within nocturnal low level jets, will aid in \nfacilitating these episodes of primarily elevated convection and \nlocally heavy rainfall. \n\nFirst batch to watch over far southern WI (as of 06z) remains on \ntrack to drop southeast through the area this morning into early \nthis afternoon with only minor adjustments made to hourly PoPs and \nQPF. This activity overall may undergo a slight weakening trending \nwith southeastward progression into areas along and southeast of the \nUS 24 corridor mid morning-early afternoon as it outpaces the \nlow level jet and associated convergence/MUCAPE axis. Maybe a \nquarter of an inch southeast to as much an inch or so up near the \nIN/MI border with this initial round. \n\nA general lull in precip coverage/chances is still anticipated for \nmost of the area mid afternoon through mid evening in between upper \nlevel impulses. Available model guidance continues to hint at \nanother shortwave and theta-e surge tonight, focusing locally heavy \nrain and embedded thunder mainly along and north of the US 30 \ncorridor. Another round then Sunday afternoon/evening possible,\nespecially if/where any boundary layer heating can occur \nresulting in heavier convective elements. Localized lowland and \nriver/stream flooding, along with flash flooding, remains a \nconcern given the saturated ground and opportunties for training\nelevated convection in this 1.8-2.0 inch PWAT air mass. No \nchanges were made to the Flood Watch for now (in effect through \nSunday morning). Latest guidance does favor areas north of the \nUS 30 corridor for higher probs for 1-3\"+ plus. Luckily lower \nprobs to the south, though any additional rain in areas just \nsouth of Fort Wayne will further exacerbate ongoing high water \nissues. \n\nSuppression of the height field and frontal zone expected Monday into \nTuesday will result in cool conditions and lower chances for \nlighter/scattered rain showers. High pressure building into the \nnorthern Great Lakes will definitely usher in some cooler and drier \nlow level air within northeasterly flow.",
